 (2)：\n\nHuang Jiaoyang\n\nAbout Huang Jiaoyang\n\n  * Search \"202450001020281470\"\n  * Search \"Huang Jiaoyang\"\n\n(Courant Institute, New York University, New York, New York, USA)\n\nAbout Courant Institute, New York University, New York, New York, USA\n\n  * Search \"201551000098035172\"\n  * Search \"Courant Institute, New York University, New York, New York, USA\"\n\n, \n\nYau Horng-Tzer\n\nAbout Yau Horng-Tzer\n\n  * Search \"202450001026096993\"\n  * Search \"Yau Horng-Tzer\"\n\n(Department of Mathematics, Harvard University, Cambridge, Massachusetts, USA)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLecture Notes on Edge Universality for Random Regular Graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.00975)\nciteturn1academia16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; The purpose of this note is to explain the structure, general strategy, and main ideas of the proof in the work of Huang, McKenzie, and Yau (2024) on the Ramanujan property and edge universality of random regular graphs. ... We first recall the local law for random $d$-regular graphs, and then illustrate the main ideas behind the derivation of the self-consistent equations and the first loop equation.\nTitle: Lecture Notes on Edge Universality for Random Regular Graphs\nAuthors: Jiaoyang Huang, Horng-Tzer Yau\nDate: Sun Feb  1 02:32:30 2026\n\nThe purpose of this note is to explain the structure, general strategy, and main ideas of the proof in the work of Huang, McKenzie, and Yau (2024) on the Ramanujan property and edge universality of random regular graphs. The core of the argument is the derivation of self-consistent equations and a microscopic version of the loop equations for random $d$-regular graphs. We first recall the local law for random $d$-regular graphs, and then illustrate the main ideas behind the derivation of the self-consistent equations and the first loop equation.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLocal Kesten–McKay law for random regular graphs (https://api.repository.cam.ac.uk/server/api/core/bitstreams/da1f80d4-630e-4906-ae71-67fec8eda55a/content)\nciteturn1search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.9 years ago; Local Kesten–McKay law for random regular graphs ... Horng-Tzer Yau‡ ... We study the adjacency matrices of random d-regular graphs with large but ﬁxed degree d.In the bulk of the spectrum [−2 ... Graph distance between switched vertices ... grant DMS-1307444, DMS-1606305 and a Simons Investigator award.\nLocal Kesten–McKay law for random regular graphs\nRoland Bauerschmidt∗\nJiaoyang Huang†\nHorng-Tzer Yau‡\nAbstract\nWe study the adjacency matrices of random d-regular graphs with large but ﬁxed degree d.\nIn the bulk of the spectrum [−2\n√\nd −1 + ε, 2\n√\nd −1 −ε] down to the optimal spectral scale, we\nprove that the Green’s functions can be approximated by those of certain inﬁnite tree-like (few\ncycles) graphs that depend only on the local structure of the original graphs. This result implies\nthat the Kesten–McKay law holds for the spectral density down to the smallest scale and the\ncomplete delocalization of bulk eigenvectors. Our method is based on estimating the Green’s\nfunction of the adjacency matrices and a resampling of the boundary edges of large balls in the\ngraphs.\nContents\n1\nMain results I: Spectral density and eigenvectors\n2\n2\nMain results II: Local approximation of the Green’s function\n6\n3\nProof outline and main ideas\n12\n4\nStructure of random and deterministic regular graphs\n16\n5\nTrees and tree extension\n19\n6\nInitial estimates\n25\n7\nLocal resampling by switching\n27\n8\nGraph distance between switched vertices\n34\n9\nThe Green’s function distance and switching cells\n38\n10 Stability under removal of a neighborhood\n41\n11 Stability under switching\n48\n12 Improved decay in the switched graph\n60\n13 Stability estimate for the switched graph\n67\n14 Concentration in the switched graph\n76\n15 Improved approximation in the switched graph\n83\n16 Proof of main results\n87\nA Combinatorial estimates for random regular graphs\n94\nB Properties of the Green’s functions\n99\n∗University of Cambridge, Statistical Laboratory, DPMMS. E-mail: rb812@cam.ac.uk.\n†Harvard University, Department of Mathematics. E-mail: jiaoyang@math.harvard.edu.\n‡Harvard University, Department of Mathematics. E-mail: htyau@math.harvard.edu, partially supported by NSF\ngrant DMS-1307444, DMS-1606305 and a Simons Investigator award.\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nJiaoyang Huang (https://jiaoyang.github.io/research.html)\nciteturn1search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 1.2 years ago; Jiaoyang Huang ... with Yukun He and Horng-Tzer Yau ...   * Spectrum of Random d-regular Graphs Up to the Edge ... Communications on Pure and Applied Mathematics, 77(3), 1635-1723, 2024. ...   * Spectral Statistics of Sparse Erdős–Rényi Graph Laplacians ...   * Local Kesten--McKay Law for Random Regular Graphs\nYukun He and Chen Wang\nPreprint, 2025.\n  * Ramanujan Property and Edge Universality of Random Regular Graphs\nwith Theo McKenzie and Horng-Tzer Yau\nPreprint, 2024.\n  * The Spectral Distribution of Random Graphs with Given Degree Sequences\nwith Shuyi Wang and Kevin Li.\nPreprint, 2024.\n  * Optimal Eigenvalue Rigidity of Random Regular Graphs,\nwith Theo McKenzie and Horng-Tzer Yau\nPermanent draft, 2024.\n  * Edge universality of random regular graphs of growing degrees\nwith Horng-Tzer Yau.\nPreprint, 2023.\n  * Edge Universality of Sparse Random Matrices\nwith Horng-Tzer Yau.\nAccepted by Annales de l'Institut Henri Poincar{\\'e} 2024.\n  * Spectrum of Random d-regular Graphs Up to the Edge\nwith Horng-Tzer Yau.\nCommunications on Pure and Applied Mathematics, 77(3), 1635-1723, 2024.\n  * Invertibility of adjacency matrices for random d-regular graphs\nDuke Mathematical Journal 170(18): 3977-4032, 2021.\n---------------------------------------------------------